        <div id="how-it-works" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How the Peloton App Works on Any Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            <strong>The Peloton App is a class library, not a treadmill controller.</strong> On a
            non-Peloton treadmill you stream a class on your phone, tablet or TV, the instructor calls out
            speeds, inclines or effort, and you press the buttons yourself. The app does not change the
            treadmill&rsquo;s speed or incline for you.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two of the picks here, the <a href="/horizon-7-0-at-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Horizon 7.0 AT</a> and T101 Connect, list Peloton among the apps they
            connect to, alongside Zwift and others, with a note that separate subscriptions are required.
            The listings do not spell out what data passes between treadmill and app, so plan as if you
            will control the machine by hand, which is how Peloton&rsquo;s classes are built for anyone
            not on a Tread.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Which membership?</strong> On Peloton&rsquo;s app pages on 21 November 2026, App+ was
            $28.99 a month or $289 a year, and App One was $12.99 a month or $129 a year, but App One limits
            you to three cardio equipment classes a month. (One older line on the same page still read $24
            for App+; the yearly saving Peloton quotes matches $28.99.)
            If treadmill classes are the point, App+ is the tier to budget for; check Peloton&rsquo;s
            current prices before you sign up. Our <a href="/how-much-does-a-peloton-tread-cost/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Peloton Tread cost guide</a> sets out what the Tread itself and its membership cost, which is the comparison that makes the
            case for an ordinary treadmill.
          </p>
