        <div id="what-to-look-for" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>What to Look For in a Treadmill for Peloton Classes</h2>
<p>
            Because you change everything by hand, the treadmill&rsquo;s controls matter more than its
            apps. In order of importance:
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li><strong>Speed and incline that cover the classes.</strong> The Peloton Tread runs to
            12.5 mph and 12.5% incline. A treadmill that reaches 12 mph and 12 to 15% will cover nearly
            every running class; one that stops at 10 mph and 10% is fine for walking, hiking and most
            jogging classes but will cap out in sprint intervals.</li>
            <li><strong>Fast ways to change speed.</strong> Instructors change pace every 30 to 60 seconds
            in interval classes. Dials, like Horizon&rsquo;s, or one-touch keys, like the Sole F50&rsquo;s
            3, 6 and 9, make that practical. Pressing an up arrow twenty times at 9 mph is not.</li>
            <li><strong>A place for the screen.</strong> A device holder or a console shelf wide enough for
            a tablet, at eye height. Without one you will be looking down at a phone on the console or
            across at a TV.</li>
            <li><strong>A deck long enough to run on.</strong> At running pace most people want 55 to 60
            inches; our <a href="/treadmill-belt-size-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">belt size guide</a> gives lengths by height and speed.</li>
            <li><strong>No second subscription.</strong> A treadmill that works fully without its own
            paid app means you only pay Peloton.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            Peloton&rsquo;s hiking and walking classes lean on incline more than speed, and the standard
            Tread has no decline. If you want downhill sections too, see our <a href="/best-treadmills-with-decline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best treadmills with decline</a>,
            and for the steepest climbs our <a href="/best-incline-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">best incline treadmills</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="class-types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Which Peloton Classes Each Treadmill Can Handle</h2>
<p>
            Peloton&rsquo;s treadmill library is not all running. Walking, hiking and bootcamp classes ask
            far less of the machine than speed intervals, so the treadmill you need depends on the classes
            you will actually take. As a rough guide:
          </p>
          <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8"><table class="min-w-[520px] w-full text-sm border-collapse">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Class type</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">What it asks of the treadmill</th>
                <th class="text-left font-black text-gray-900 border-b border-gray-200 py-2 pr-4">Picks that handle it comfortably</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Walking</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Low speeds, gentle incline, a stable deck</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">All six</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Hiking</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Sustained incline; 10% or more helps</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">All six; the Aeriflo, Sole F65 and Horizon 7.0 AT go furthest</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Bootcamp</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Frequent on-and-off; a safety key you can trust and quick restarts</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">All six</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Endurance and tempo runs</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Steady running speed on a long deck</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Horizon 7.0 AT, T202, Sole F65 (60-inch decks)</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Speed intervals</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Fast changes up to 10 to 12 mph</td><td class="border-b border-gray-100 py-2 pr-4 text-gray-700">Horizon 7.0 AT, T202, Sole F65, Aeriflo</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table></div>
          <p>
            Bootcamp classes deserve a word of caution. They alternate treadmill intervals with floor
            exercises, so you step on and off a moving or just-stopped belt many times. Stop the belt fully
            before you step off, and keep the floor beside the treadmill clear. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-get-on-and-off-a-treadmill-safely/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">getting on and off a treadmill safely</a> covers the routine. If you mainly walk and hike, a 10 mph treadmill such as the T101 Connect
            or Sole F50 is plenty, and the money saved can go on a better tablet mount or a fan; if you plan
            to work through the running programmes, buy for the fastest class you expect to take, not the
            slowest.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="vs-tread" class="scroll-mt-24">
