        <div id="setup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Setting Up Peloton Classes on Your Treadmill</h2>
<ol>
            <li><strong>Put the screen at eye height.</strong> Use the device holder or console shelf for
            a tablet, or mount a TV in front of the treadmill. Looking down for 30 minutes strains the neck
            and your form.</li>
            <li><strong>Learn your speed numbers first.</strong> Walk and jog a few minutes on the treadmill
            alone and note the speeds that feel easy, moderate and hard. Peloton classes give targets, and
            knowing your own numbers makes them quicker to hit. Our <a href="/treadmill-pace-and-speed-chart/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">pace and speed chart</a> converts pace to mph.</li>
            <li><strong>Use a heart-rate strap if you want metrics.</strong> The app can read a Bluetooth
            heart-rate monitor directly, which gives you effort data even when the treadmill sends nothing.</li>
            <li><strong>Start with walking or hiking classes.</strong> They have fewer and slower changes,
            which lets you practise using the controls before you try sprint intervals.</li>
            <li><strong>Clip on the safety key.</strong> Watching a screen and pressing buttons at speed is
            the moment to have it attached.</li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>4 Mistakes People Make Buying a Treadmill for the Peloton App</h2>
          <p>Most of these come from expecting a regular treadmill to behave like a Tread.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Expecting the app to change the speed</h3>
              <p>On a non-Peloton treadmill, the instructor calls the speed and you press the button. Buy a machine with dials or quick keys so that keeping up is easy, rather than one with only up and down arrows.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying a 10 mph treadmill for running classes</h3>
              <p>Peloton’s Tread goes to 12.5 mph. If you plan to take running and interval classes, choose a machine that reaches 12 mph; 10 mph machines suit walking, hiking and jogging classes.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Forgetting the screen</h3>
              <p>Without a device holder or a console shelf wide enough for a tablet, you end up looking down at a phone. Check where your screen will sit, or plan a wall-mounted TV in front of the treadmill.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Paying for two subscriptions</h3>
              <p>A treadmill built around iFIT or JRNY pushes you toward its own membership. If Peloton is your class library, pick a machine that works fully without its own paid app.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

